          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            One can view a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+measure"} probability measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            on a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\mathcal{A})
            as a “pile of mass”, for example, of sand, on the space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\mathcal{A},p)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y,\mathcal{B},q)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            in such a way that the sand from the pile
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is arranged to form the pile
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            = succeed "," do
              %em transport plan
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernel"} Markov kernel
            (see below).
          %p
            It is useful to keep track of
            %em in which way
